 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+#### Keep on top — Experimental
+
+`manager.setAlwaysOnTop( windowId, enabled )` turns **Keep on top** on or off for a window; the title bar's pin button (`core/always-on-top`) calls the same method. `window.alwaysOnTop` reads it back.
+
+```javascript
+const { windowManager } = wp.os;
+windowManager.setAlwaysOnTop( 'my-plugin-timer', true );
+windowManager.getById( 'my-plugin-timer' ).alwaysOnTop; // true
+```
+
+- A kept-on-top window is **drawn** above every window that is not, even when one of those is focused or newly opened. Focus itself is unchanged: clicking a window underneath focuses it (and `getFocused()` returns it) without covering the pinned one.
+- Several windows can be kept on top; among themselves they stack in normal focus order.
+- A child window is drawn in its owner's band, so it is never under an owner that is kept on top.
+- A fullscreen window still covers everything.
+- Unknown ids, and setting the value a window already has, are no-ops. A real change fires `os-window-always-on-top-changed` and the `os.window.always-on-top-changed` action with `{ windowId, alwaysOnTop }`, and the window carries the `os-window--always-on-top` class while it is on.
+- The flag is saved with the session (`alwaysOnTop: true` on the window's entry) and comes back after a reload.
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
